---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi2_20",
  "series": "Band VI 2 (1663-1672); durchgesehener Nachdruck 1990",
  "seq": 20,
  "text": "[[387.51]] <b>51. Vitia Disputationis Confusaneae  </b> <span>387.0;0:62:051:6805-7112:L:l^</span>.\n[[387.1]] Vitia Disputationis Confusaneae:\n[[387.2]] (1.) Confusanea Disputatio est in qua forma ratiocinandi non servatur. Et in ea\n[[387.3]] vel conclusio facienda non est, vel facienda est.\n[[387.4]] (2.) Si conclusio facienda non est, inutilis est disputatio, nec nisi oblectamentum\n[[387.5]] tendit; sin conclusio facienda est tunc aut numeranda aut ponderanda vota sunt. Et disputare\n[[387.6]] licet, vel non licet.\n[[387.7]] (3.) Si numeranda sunt vota tunc aut omnes informati sunt, aut non sunt. Si omnes\n[[387.8]] informati sunt, et numeranda sunta vota, et disputare non licet, tunc frustra afferuntur\n[[387.9]] rationes a votante. Neque enim spes est facile alium rationibus suis a concepta sententia\n[[387.10]] abducturum; suffecerit ergo sententiam simpliciter unumquemque rogari. Et ab eo\n[[387.11]] responderi sine ulla licet ratione. Cum frustra sit ratiocinari.\n[[387.12]] (4.) Sin omnes informati non sunt, sed quidam tantum, qui caeteros informare debent,\n[[387.13]] et numeranda sunt vota, et disputare non licet: Tunc difficillime caeteri non informati\n[[387.14]] judicare possunt utrique ex informatis si dissentiunt, aut si consentiunt, utrum omnino\n[[387.15]] eorum sententia sit vera si quidem oretenus res determinanda est e vestigio.\n[[387.16]] (5.) Nam si determinatio tempus fert, tunc licet caeteros annotare in charta quae\n[[387.17]] audiunt (ideo enim eis charta proponitur) et expendere deinde per otium quanquam sic\n[[387.18]] quoque forte omissuri sunt et oretenus dicentes pleraque in proponendo, et audientes\n[[387.19]] pleraque et forte optima in excipiendo. Sin in scriptis res proponenda, tunc res solet\n[[387.20]] lente admodum et tarde ob molestiam legendi, explicari.\n[[388.1]] (6.) Caeteri jam informandi si consentiunt post informationem res manifesta est, sin\n[[388.2]] dissentiunt[,] res redit in eum statum quo omnes informati, aut si nova afferunt, habendi\n[[388.3]] ipsi pro informatis, caeteri pro informandis, et ita secundum priora.\n[[388.4]] (7.) Si numeranda sunt vota, et disputare licet, tunc aut director adest, qui directorio\n[[388.5]] suo utitur, aut non adest. Si director non adest tunc inutilis est omnis disputatio[,] si\n[[388.6]] quidem numeranda non ponderanda sunt vota, neque enim quisquam facile in publica\n[[388.7]] ejusmodi collatione nisi accedant secretae rationes aut singularia insueta artificia, ab opinione\n[[388.8]] sua ad opinionem alterius transit, praesertim in juridicis, atque plerumque nihil refert\n[[388.9]] magnopere disputantium an et quomodo sequatur conclusio, quod secus plerumque est\n[[388.10]] in deliberationibus politicis, ubi proprium interesse necessitat saepe ad deserendam\n[[388.11]] opinionem suam et mittendam pertinaciam. Praetera si director abest, tunc aut replicare et\n[[388.12]] disputare licet in infinitum aut alteri statim ab initio dicere licet: dixi sententiam meam,\n[[388.13]] dic tu tuam, non est cur disputemus, nisi forte ut alios informet, replicat, ne illi ab altero\n[[388.14]] occupentur.\n[[388.15]] (8.) Si igitur huius rei causa replicatur tunc vel replicatur interuptis verbis vel\n[[388.16]] continuis quasi perorationibus. Si replicatur interruptis verbis, seu interpellare alterum licet\n[[388.17]] tunc haec mala sequuntur 1) quod non licet pertexcre telam rationis suae, 2) quod\n[[388.18]] obliviscimur aliarum rationum dum de hac altercamus, 3) quod etsi velimus subnectere et\n[[388.19]] caeteras, taedium disputationis primae facile adimit reliquis audiendi caeteras\n[[388.20]] voluptatem, et per consequens attentionem, 4) auditores in illa inordinata reciprocatione omnium\n[[388.21]] hinc inde tandem obliviscuntur, aut confunduntur; et ita cessat fructus tum convincendi\n[[388.22]] adversarium, cui ab uno ad aliud transsilire, eludereque ratiocinantem, et rem in infinitum\n[[388.23]] protrahere licet; tum persuadendi caeteros, confusos aut taedio affectos.\n[[388.24]] (9.) Sin disputatur continuis perorationibus tunc cessat quidem unum malum, licet\n[[388.25]] enim hic cuilibet suam telam pertexere. Subit vero alterum, quod rationes rationibus non\n[[388.26]] opponuntur, vir viro non occuritur, pes pedi non conseritur. Licet enim cuilibet transire\n[[388.27]] optima adversarii, affere denuo centies refutata, ut taceam et ipsum et auditores mutuo\n[[388.28]] allatorum oblivisci, et abire etiam replicationes in infinitum.\n[[388.29]] (10.) Species disputationis continuis perorationibus factae est quae in scriptis fit,\n[[388.30]] sed habet commodum prae aliis, quod oblivio dictorum irrepere non potest, sed omnia\n[[388.31]] regulari possunt. Sed contra succedit taedium legentium in ista longitudine, quia necessario\n[[388.32]] omnia in scriptis longiora et ita tandem etiam oblivio ut ex innumeris exemplis eorum patet\n[[388.33]] qui libris editis invicem decertarunt. Accedit hoc malum, quod in scriptis uberior est potestas\n[[388.34]] prolixitatis et aliena afferendi quam viva voce, quia in sermone vivo judicium audientium\n[[388.35]] vereri debes, antequam te absolvisti, quod non est in scriptis, quia litera non erubescit.\n[[389.1]] (11.) Si quis director adest, id est in cujus potestate est quaestiones formare ut\n[[389.2]] lubet et disputationem interrumpere et finire ubi lubet (etiamsi autoritatem alios movendi\n[[389.3]] non habeat, de hac enim nolo hic loqui) tunc posito quod is director sit prudens et bonus,\n[[389.4]] res exitum reperire possunt. Director enim vel ab initio format quaestiones\n[[389.5]] praeliminares, vel eas format ex disceptationibus partium. Et quotiescunque nova affertur ratio,\n[[389.6]] suspendit numerationem majorum et de illa ratione quaerit. Et omnino cavere potest\n[[389.7]] ne ulla ratio sine refutatione relinquatur, quia de rationibus rationum quasi novis\n[[389.8]] quaestionibus emergentibus semper quaestiones formare, et impedire potest, ne quid sicco pede\n[[389.9]] praetereatur. Si quis igitur detur director bonus et sagax, tunc spes est pleraque recte\n[[389.10]] confici posse. Sed directores plerumque sunt parum sagaces et periti; interdum vero sagaces\n[[389.11]] sed mali, et utuntur arte illa formandi quaestiones vel praeliminares vel emergentes,\n[[389.12]] tantum in rebus sibi utilibus, vel in quas privato quodam affectu feruntur. Et ita justitia\n[[389.13]] ab eorum voluntate pendet, quod miserum est admodum.\n[[389.14]] (12.) Et est omnino his modis omnibus commune, quod casu tantum pervenitur ad\n[[389.15]] decisionem eamque tam saepe justam quam injustam. Nam plerumque rationes aut\n[[389.16]] ultimae aut primae aut peculiari quadam circumstantia prolatae solent potissimum\n[[389.17]] animis infixae manere, et a directore aliisve aripi; et si taedium obrepit longae\n[[389.18]] disputationis aeque saepe minuta quam solida ratio fit decisiva. In bonitate autem et prudentia\n[[389.19]] directoris solum confidere a justitiae pariter et politicae rationibus alienum est.\n[[389.20]] (13.) Denique omnino injustum est numerari vota praesertim officialium. Tolerabilior\n[[389.21]] est numeratio haec quando Assessores sunt Interessentes et de ipsorum corio luditur, ut in\n[[389.22]] societatibus. At hic principis aut boni publici res agitur. Cur enim teneatur se princeps,\n[[389.23]] aut director nomine principis conformare majoribus; quasi et princeps aliquis ex Assessoribus\n[[389.24]] esset, si diis placet.\n[[389.25]] (14.) Ponderanda igitur sunt vota, sed ita res abibit in disputationes sine fine. Ita\n[[389.26]] sane, si res nullis regulis includatur. At potest res regulis ita firmis solidisque includi ut\n[[389.27]] aeque celeriter quam alias, et solide tuteque sine periculo errandi ex omnibus dubiis\n[[389.28]] emergere liceat, et nihil possit omitti, et nihil possit bis dici, et nulla ratio sine responsione,\n[[389.29]] et nulla responsio si patitur sine replicatione praetermittatur, et a nulla re nisi confecta\n[[389.30]] ad aliam transsiliatur, et nihil sine probatione dicatur. et nihil nisi formaliter concludatur.\n",
  "number": "51.",
  "title": "Vitia Disputationis Confusaneae",
  "date": ""
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:51. Vitia Disputationis Confusaneae 387.0;0:62:051:6805-7112:L:l^. Vitia Disputationis Confusaneae: (1.) Confusanea Disputatio est in qua forma ratiocinandi non servatur. Et in ea vel conclusio facienda non est, vel facienda est. (2.) Si conclusio facienda non est, inutilis est disputatio, nec nisi ...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>